<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krzeszów Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1292</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krzeszów Abbey is a former Cistercian abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the Czech border. Founded in 1242 for Benedictines and refounded for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-the-strict">Bolko I the Strict</a> in 1292, it became the burial place of the Piast dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swidnica">Świdnica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawor">Jawor</a>. Its 18th-century baroque church and ducal mausoleum are among the finest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krzeszow-abbey.jpg" alt="Krzeszów Abbey" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scotch Mist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krzeszow_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
